On January 15, 1919, people in Boston's North End heard a sound like machine-gun fire. The rivets of a huge steel tank near the harbor were popping loose. The tank {{1|held: well over}} two million gallons of molasses, and within seconds all of it was pouring into the streets.

The molasses belonged to a company that turned it into industrial alcohol{{2|. Which was used}} to make explosives. The tank, about fifty feet tall, {{3|has leaked}} almost from the day it was first filled. Neighbors had collected the dripping molasses for their kitchens, and the company had simply painted the tank brown so that the leaks would be harder to see.{{4|}}

Because the day was unusually {{5|warm the}} molasses flowed more freely than it would have in colder weather. A wave of it, in places about twenty-five feet high, {{6|moved through the neighborhood}}. When the molasses reached the elevated railway, {{7|it buckled its steel supports}}. The flood killed twenty-one people, injured about 150 others, and {{8|it also flattened}} several buildings.

[1] The company claimed that someone had blown up the tank on purpose. [2] A court-appointed investigator then heard testimony from thousands of witnesses over several years. [3] In 1925, he found the company responsible {{9|for}} the disaster, concluding that the tank had been too weak to hold its load. [4] The case helped persuade Massachusetts to require that plans for such structures be checked and signed by a licensed engineer. {{10|}}
